  "full_response": "WebSockets and HTTP long polling are both techniques used to achieve real-time communication between a client (e.g., a web browser) and a server, but they work in fundamentally different ways. Here’s a breakdown of each and their key differences:\n\n---\n\n### **1. WebSockets**\n**What it is:**\nWebSocket is a **full-duplex, bidirectional communication protocol** that operates over a single, persistent TCP connection. It was standardized in 2011 (RFC 6455) and is designed for real-time, low-latency communication.\n\n**How it works:**\n- The client initiates a WebSocket handshake via an HTTP `Upgrade` request (e.g., `GET /chat` with headers like `Upgrade: websocket`).\n- If the server supports WebSockets, it responds with `HTTP 101 Switching Protocols`, and the connection upgrades from HTTP to WebSocket.\n- After the handshake, the connection remains open, allowing **both the client and server to send messages at any time** without re-establishing the connection.\n- Data is framed in small chunks (messages), and the protocol supports text (UTF-8) and binary data.\n\n**Key Features:**\n- **Persistent connection:** No need to repeatedly open/close connections.\n- **Low latency:** Messages are sent immediately without waiting for a request.\n- **Efficient:** Minimal overhead (just a few bytes per message) after the initial handshake.\n- **Bidirectional:** Both client and server can push data independently.\n- **Event-driven:** Ideal for apps like chat, live updates, gaming, or stock tickers.\n\n**Example Use Cases:**\n- Real-time chat apps (e.g., Slack, Discord).\n- Live sports/financial updates.\n- Multiplayer online games.\n- Collaborative tools (e.g., Google Docs).\n\n**Limitations:**\n- Not all servers/proxies support WebSockets (though most modern ones do).\n- Requires handling connection drops and reconnections.\n- More complex to implement than HTTP for simple use cases.\n\n---\n\n### **2. HTTP Long Polling**\n**What it is:**\nLong polling is a **workaround to simulate real-time communication** using standard HTTP requests. It’s not a separate protocol but a technique built on top of HTTP.\n\n**How it works:**\n1. The client sends an HTTP request to the server (e.g., `GET /updates`).\n2. The server **holds the request open**",
